 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "YR WYDDFA. 1,085 m, highest peak in Wales. Please add one stone to the cairn. Do not take one. We counted."
  },
  {
   "x": 55,
   "y": 40,
   "text": "PYG TRACK / MINERS' TRACK / LLANBERIS PATH. Three ways up, one way to regret it: wearing the wrong socks."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "CAUTION: summit ahead, fog likely, views unlikely. NIWL / MIST. You'll get used to it."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "CLOGWYN: where the railway stops and the knees begin."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 35,
   "text": "LLYN LLYDAW. Water the colour of a very expensive jade ornament. Arthur's sword went in nearby, allegedly."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "SUMMIT: LOOSE SCREE ONLY. If you cannot fell-scramble, do not. If you can, do so with dignity."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "yr_wyddfa_rhita_gawr",
   "title": "Rhita Gawr",
   "text": "Legend says the giant Rhita Gawr wore a cloak made from the beards of kings he had beaten, and demanded King Arthur's beard to finish it. Arthur declined, defeated him, and had him buried under a great cairn on the summit. Hence Yr Wyddfa, the burial mound."
  },
  {
   "x": 51,
   "y": 23,
   "plaque": "yr_wyddfa_first_ascent",
   "title": "First Ascent",
   "text": "The first recorded climb of Yr Wyddfa was in 1639, by the botanist Thomas Johnson, who came for the plants rather than the view. Today hundreds of thousands of people a year reach the top, many warming up in Hafod Eryri, the summit visitor centre opened in 2009."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 13,
   "plaque": "landsky_wyddfa_volcano",
   "title": "Fossils on the summit",
   "text": "The rocks of Yr Wyddfa are ash and lava from volcanoes that erupted about 450 million years ago, when Wales lay under a sea south of the equator. Shells of sea creatures settled between the eruptions, which is why there are fossils near the summit of the highest mountain in Wales. Ice did the rest: in the ice ages, glaciers scooped the great hollows called cwms and left the lakes, Glaslyn and Llyn Llydaw, in the bottom of them."
  }
 ],
